---
import Page from '../layouts/Page.astro';
import { CONTACT_EMAIL, GITHUB_URL, LEGAL_UPDATED } from '../config';
---

<Page
  title="Privacy Policy | AnyAirDrop.com"
  description="What AnyAirDrop.com does and does not collect: no accounts, no ads, no trackers, and your files are never stored on our servers."
  heading="Privacy"
  lead="AnyAirDrop.com is built to need as little of your information as possible. This page explains exactly what is involved when you use it."
  updated={LEGAL_UPDATED}
>
  <h2>The short version</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>There are no accounts, no ads, no analytics and no tracking scripts.</li>
    <li>Your files are never uploaded to or stored on our servers. They go from one device to the other.</li>
    <li>
      Our server only introduces your devices to each other. It briefly handles a device name, the kind of device and
      browser, and connection details, and keeps no database of users or transfers.
    </li>
    <li>Everything that is kept lives on your own devices, and you can delete it at any time.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Your files</h2>
  <p>
    When two devices can reach each other, your browser connects directly to the other browser, and the files travel
    between them over an encrypted connection. They do not pass through our server, and their names and contents are never
    sent to it.
  </p>
  <p>
    If a network blocks direct connections, the transfer can go through an encrypted relay run by Cloudflare. The relay
    forwards the encrypted data without being able to read it, and nothing is stored there. The page tells you when this is
    happening. Because a relay moves your data over the internet, it counts towards your mobile or home data allowance.
  </p>
  <p>Nothing starts until the receiving person taps Accept, unless they have chosen to accept files from a trusted device automatically.</p>

  <h2>What our server handles while you use the site</h2>
  <p>To introduce two devices, a small signaling service handles the following while your page is open:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>A device name: a random one like “Sage Finch”, or the name you chose.</li>
    <li>The kind of device (phone, tablet or computer), its operating system and its browser.</li>
    <li>A random identifier created for your tab, used only to route messages between devices.</li>
    <li>
      A way to place devices in the same room: either a one-way hash of your network’s public address (so devices on the
      same Wi-Fi find each other), a six-digit pairing code, or the secret key of a trusted device.
    </li>
    <li>The connection details browsers exchange to open a direct connection, which include network addresses.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    This information is held in memory while your device is connected and is gone when you close the page. We do not write
    it to a database, and we do not build profiles. The hash is used as a room name and is not kept anywhere else by our
    code. A hash of an address is not a perfect shield, so please treat the shared-network matching as something a
    determined person could undo.
  </p>

  <h2>What the other device learns</h2>
  <p>
    A direct connection works by the two devices exchanging their network addresses. The device you connect to will see your
    device name, the kind of device and browser, and the network addresses needed to connect. If that matters to you, use a
    pairing code only with people you know, and remember that a VPN changes the address that is shared.
  </p>

  <h2>Companies that help run the site</h2>
  <p>AnyAirDrop.com relies on two kinds of provider, each with its own privacy policy:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Cloudflare</strong> provides the domain’s DNS, the protection and delivery in front of the site, the
      signaling service and the relay. Like any such provider it processes technical data such as IP addresses and request
      details, and may keep standard logs and set technical security cookies. See
      <a href="https://www.cloudflare.com/privacypolicy/">Cloudflare’s privacy policy</a>.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Our web hosting provider</strong> serves the pages and keeps standard server logs, for example your IP
      address, the time, the page requested and your browser.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>We do not control how long those providers keep their logs, and we do not use these logs to identify or follow visitors.</p>

  <h2>Cookies and data on your device</h2>
  <p>AnyAirDrop.com does not set cookies of its own. It uses your browser’s storage for these things:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Kept until you clear it:</strong> your light or dark theme, a device name you chose, your list of trusted devices (each holds a secret key), and whether trusted devices are added automatically.</li>
    <li><strong>Kept until you close the tab:</strong> a random device identifier, your pairing code and your temporary device name.</li>
    <li>
      <strong>Temporary file space:</strong> large incoming files are written to your browser’s private storage while they
      arrive, and are cleaned up afterwards.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    You can remove any of this from your browser’s settings. Trusted devices can also be removed one by one from the
    Trusted list under the radar.
  </p>

  <h2>Trusted devices</h2>
  <p>
    When two devices trust each other, they share a long secret key stored on both. It lets them find each other on any
    network. Anyone who obtains the key could join that private room and see that those two devices are online, so
    protect the devices you use, and remove a trusted device if you lose access to it. A trusted device cannot send you
    files without your approval unless you turn that on for it. You can switch off the automatic adding of devices in the
    Trusted list.
  </p>

  <h2>If you write to us</h2>
  <p>
    If you email <a href={`mailto:${CONTACT_EMAIL}`}>{CONTACT_EMAIL}</a>, we receive your address and your message, and use
    them only to answer you. We keep the conversation for as long as it is useful, and delete it when you ask. Please
    never attach private files or passwords.
  </p>

  <h2>Your rights</h2>
  <p>
    Because there are no accounts and we keep no user database, there is usually nothing about you that we can look up.
    You still have the rights your local law gives you, such as asking what we hold, asking us to correct or delete it,
    and complaining to a data protection authority. Write to us and we will help as far as we can.
  </p>

  <h2>Children</h2>
  <p>AnyAirDrop.com is not aimed at children under 13, and we do not knowingly collect information from them.</p>

  <h2>Changes</h2>
  <p>
    If this page changes, the date at the top changes too. The code that does all of this is open for anyone to read on
    <a href={GITHUB_URL}>GitHub</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>Contact</h2>
  <p>Questions about privacy: <a href={`mailto:${CONTACT_EMAIL}`}>{CONTACT_EMAIL}</a>, or see the <a href="/contact/">contact page</a>.</p>
</Page>
